Sixth, route admission, for a host that decides the route of each
connection itself, as one that follows the system's proxy settings
does. While a session requires it, each connection the session opens
itself (a TCP connection, a racer, an HTTP link, an Auto HTTP probe and
a TCP recheck) waits, right before its connect and after its address
and a named proxy host are picked, until the host admits its
destination: the session reports EngineEvent::RouteRequired once per
destination, with an id that only grows, the transport (TCP or HTTP)
and the destination's host and port as the session names them (a
datacenter address, never the proxy), and asks again under a new id
after 30 s unanswered. Waiting counts as no failure, raises no proxy
connection issues and starts no Auto HTTP probe. One admission opens
one attempt to exactly its destination, in the drive after the answer;
one not used there is dropped, so a later attempt asks again. An answer
wakes every connection that waited for it; a refusal is a failed
attempt of the one that asked, with its usual backoff. A pause and a
different proxy, address list, HTTP address list, transport,
obfuscation id or admission setting void what is pending or admitted,
and the connections that waited ask anew. Streams the host opens itself
(StreamHost) are not gated. Off, the default, nothing changes.
Contract tests pin what a host that moves a session between proxies
relies on: a paused session given a new proxy connects only after
unpause and through it, a call outstanding across a paused proxy switch
runs and completes once, and an HTTP proxy that refuses CONNECT carries
Auto over forwarded HTTP to the host's HTTP addresses. The test
server's binary gains --http-proxy both|forward-only and
--http-proxy-credentials user:password, for the HTTP proxy front its
library already has. Tests:
